WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.954
Keep the acid identity, concentration,
volume, and temperature comparable, and

00:00:00.955 --> 00:00:03.157
consider the metal's exposed surface.

00:00:03.474 --> 00:00:06.339
Otherwise, faster fizzing might
reflect different conditions

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:07.946
rather than just the metal itself

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:12.133
For the metal oxides and hydroxides
in our course, reacting them with

00:00:12.133 --> 00:00:14.374
an acid produces a salt and water.

00:00:14.739 --> 00:00:16.343
This is neutralization.

00:00:16.761 --> 00:00:21.615
Hydrochloric acid plus sodium hydroxide
gives sodium chloride and water.

00:00:22.373 --> 00:00:27.457
So any amount of any acid mixed with
any amount of alkali gives pH seven.

00:00:28.179 --> 00:00:28.702
Easy.

00:00:29.476 --> 00:00:30.221
Too easy.

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:34.502
If acid remains in excess, the
final solution can still be acidic.

00:00:34.744 --> 00:00:37.413
If alkali is in excess,
it can be alkaline.

00:00:37.739 --> 00:00:41.872
The amounts matter, and some salt
solutions are not neutral even when the

00:00:41.872 --> 00:00:44.041
reactants are in the required proportions.

00:00:44.819 --> 00:00:50.552
In the acid-alkali example, hydrogen ions
and hydroxide ions combine to make water.

00:00:51.012 --> 00:00:54.613
I must not assume the endpoint
from the word neutralization alone.

00:00:55.228 --> 00:00:56.006
Exactly.

00:00:56.284 --> 00:00:59.813
Indicators or pH measurements
help us follow what happens.

00:01:00.166 --> 00:01:04.012
Farmers can use measured amounts
of lime to reduce soil acidity, but

00:01:04.012 --> 00:01:05.864
more is not automatically better.

00:01:06.319 --> 00:01:08.678
The aim is a suitable
condition for the crop.

00:01:09.409 --> 00:01:12.626
How do I know the salt's
name rather than invent one?

00:01:13.379 --> 00:01:17.077
In these metal salt examples, the
first part comes from the metal.

00:01:17.605 --> 00:01:21.733
Hydrochloric acid makes chlorides,
sulfuric acid makes sulfates,

00:01:22.023 --> 00:01:23.995
and nitric acid makes nitrates.

00:01:24.407 --> 00:01:28.838
Copper oxide reacting with sulfuric
acid forms copper sulfate and water.

00:01:29.558 --> 00:01:32.716
Sodium hydrochloride would
not be the correct name.

00:01:33.061 --> 00:01:35.186
It is sodium chloride.

00:01:35.936 --> 00:01:36.495
Correct.

00:01:36.639 --> 00:01:40.300
Naming patterns help, but product
rules still depend on the reaction.

00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:44.727
Nitric acid has special oxidizing
behavior, so we must not assume

00:01:44.728 --> 00:01:48.195
it always follows the simple
metal plus acid hydrogen pattern

00:01:48.625 --> 00:01:53.208
A suitable reactive metal, such as
magnesium, reacts with dilute hydrochloric

00:01:53.213 --> 00:01:55.534
acid to make a salt and hydrogen.

00:01:56.046 --> 00:01:59.368
How is that different from reacting
a metal carbonate with the acid?

00:02:00.139 --> 00:02:03.508
A metal carbonate gives carbon
dioxide instead, and there

00:02:03.509 --> 00:02:05.402
is water as well as the salt.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:06.891
Exactly.

00:02:07.205 --> 00:02:11.663
Magnesium plus hydrochloric acid
forms magnesium chloride and hydrogen.

00:02:12.213 --> 00:02:16.131
Calcium carbonate plus hydrochloric
acid forms calcium chloride,

00:02:16.298 --> 00:02:18.189
water, and carbon dioxide.

00:02:18.804 --> 00:02:21.749
The word carbonate changes
the predicted products.

00:02:22.454 --> 00:02:24.969
Bubbles alone cannot
tell me which gas it is.

00:02:25.429 --> 00:02:29.184
I need the reactants and,
if appropriate, a gas test.

00:02:29.938 --> 00:02:33.383
In a teacher-managed small-scale
test, hydrogen gives a

00:02:33.383 --> 00:02:35.205
squeaky pop with a lit splint.

00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:39.154
We describe the observation for
the exam, not try to produce or

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:41.010
collect flammable gas ourselves.

00:02:41.751 --> 00:02:43.362
Lit splint for hydrogen.

00:02:43.825 --> 00:02:48.049
Oxygen uses a glowing splint
that relights, not the same test.

00:02:48.833 --> 00:02:49.202
Good.

00:02:49.696 --> 00:02:51.314
Oxygen supports combustion.

00:02:51.694 --> 00:02:54.966
A glowing splint relighting
is the familiar oxygen test.

00:02:55.374 --> 00:02:57.438
Carbon dioxide is tested differently.

00:02:57.725 --> 00:03:01.339
Passing it through limewater makes
the limewater cloudy or milky.

00:03:02.078 --> 00:03:02.984
The cloudiness

00:03:03.013 --> 00:03:07.023
is a fine solid precipitate,
calcium carbonate, not

00:03:07.023 --> 00:03:07.258
just a

00:03:07.258 --> 00:03:07.489
few

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:09.311
bubbles making the liquid look pale.

00:03:10.107 --> 00:03:10.638
Correct.

00:03:10.909 --> 00:03:13.165
Keep the test and observation
paired accurately.

00:03:13.691 --> 00:03:17.544
Never taste limewater, inhale
unknown gases, or use your mouth to

00:03:17.545 --> 00:03:19.256
draw chemicals through apparatus.

00:03:19.583 --> 00:03:22.242
Fire and gas tests need
appropriate supervision.

00:03:22.947 --> 00:03:24.354
Hydrogen, pop.

00:03:24.892 --> 00:03:27.406
Oxygen, glowing splint relights.

00:03:27.791 --> 00:03:30.282
Carbon dioxide, cloudy limewater.

00:03:30.866 --> 00:03:33.768
I can remember the three without
doing a dangerous kitchen show

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:38.488
Our simplified metal reactivity
series runs potassium, sodium,

00:03:38.685 --> 00:03:44.662
calcium, magnesium, aluminium, zinc,
iron, then hydrogen as a reference,

00:03:44.881 --> 00:03:47.282
followed by copper, silver, and gold.

00:03:47.708 --> 00:03:51.678
Which side of hydrogen predicts
reaction with a suitable dilute acid?

00:03:52.465 --> 00:03:56.548
Metals above hydrogen can release
hydrogen from suitable dilute acids.

00:03:56.871 --> 00:03:59.177
Metals below it generally do not.

00:03:59.942 --> 00:04:04.657
Correct for our usual dilute hydrochloric
or sulfuric acid comparisons.

00:04:05.194 --> 00:04:09.742
Magnesium often reacts briskly, zinc
more steadily, and iron more slowly.

00:04:10.154 --> 00:04:13.658
Copper does not release hydrogen from
those acids under ordinary conditions.

00:04:14.445 --> 00:04:17.231
But we already said nitric
acid can behave differently.

00:04:17.766 --> 00:04:21.088
Copper does not mean no reaction
with every possible acid.

00:04:21.793 --> 00:04:22.614
Exactly.

00:04:22.961 --> 00:04:26.981
Surface coatings, temperature, and
other factors affect observed rates.

00:04:27.378 --> 00:04:30.836
Aluminium's oxide layer can slow
an apparent reaction even though

00:04:30.866 --> 00:04:32.609
aluminium is high in the series.

00:04:33.064 --> 00:04:36.851
Do not replace evidence with a
universal faster fizzing slogan.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:39.063
What happens with water

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:40.000
rather than acid?
